/*!
    \page vscodeext-how-profile-qml.html
    \previouspage vscodeext-how-tos.html
    \nextpage vscodeext-reference.html

    \ingroup vscodeext-how-to-qml

    \title Profile QML code

    With the QML Profiler integrated in \QC, you can find causes for typical
    performance problems in Qt Quick applications, such as slowness and
    unresponsive, stuttering user interfaces. You can open profiling data in
    \QVSC and view it as a flame graph.

    \section1 Collect profiling data in \QC

    Use \QC to profile the application and save QML trace files in the \c {.qzt}
    or \c {.qdt} format in the project directory.

    For more information, see \l {\QC: Profile QML applications}.

    \section1 View profiling data

    To view profiling data in VS Code, open a \c {.qzt} or \c {.qdt} file in the
    \uicontrol Explorer.

    A flame graph shows a concise statistical overview of QML and JavaScript
    execution and the total impact of the various QML and JavaScript events.
    However, it does not show the time spans when no QML or JavaScript is
    running at all, so you cannot use it to analyze per frame execution times.

    Generally, events in the flame graph indicate how long QML or JavaScript
    execution took. Move the mouse over them to see details. For most events,
    they include location in source code, duration and some relevant parts of
    the source code itself.

    Select an event to move the cursor in the code editor to the part
    of the code the event is associated with.

    \section2 View total time

    In \uicontrol {Total time}, the horizontal bars show the amount of
    time all invocations of a certain function took together, relative to the
    total runtime of all JavaScript and QML events. The nesting shows which
    functions were called by which other ones.

    \image {vscodeext-qml-profiler-total-time.webp} {Total time view}

    \section2 View total memory allocation

    To view the total amount of memory allocated by the functions, select
    \uicontrol {Memory}.

    \section2 View memory allocation by function

    To view the number of memory allocations performed by the functions,
    select \uicontrol {Allocations}.

    \section2 Zoom the data

    To zoom to fit, select \inlineimage {zoom-in.png} {Zoom to fit}
    (\uicontrol {Zoom to fit}) or double-click an event.

    To zoom out to the parent event, select
    \inlineimage {zoom-out.png} {Zoom out to parent}
    (\uicontrol {Zoom out to parent}).

    To zoom out to the full view, select
    \inlineimage {fittoview.png} {Zoom out to full view}
    (\uicontrol {Zoom out to full view}).

    \section1 Filter events

    To filter events:

    \list 1
        \li Select \inlineimage {filter.png} {Show/hide filter dialog}
            (\uicontrol {Show/hide filter dialog}).
        \li Select event categories in the list to show and hide events.
    \endlist

    Select \uicontrol {Select all} to show all events and \uicontrol {Clear} to
    hide all events.

    \section2 Events

    The following table describes the events.

    \table
    \header
        \li Event
        \li Description
    \row
        \li \uicontrol JavaScript
        \li Displays the time spent executing the actual JavaScript behind
            bindings and signal handlers. Lists all the JavaScript functions
            you may be using to evaluate bindings or handle signals.
    \row
        \li \uicontrol Compiling
        \li Displays the time spent compiling the QML files.
    \row
        \li \uicontrol Creating
        \li Displays the time spent creating the elements in the scene.
            The creation of elements takes place in two stages. The first
            stage is for the creation of the data structures, including child
            elements. The second stage represents the completion callbacks. Not
            all elements trigger completion callbacks, though. The stages are
            shown as separate events in the timeline.
    \row
        \li \uicontrol Binding
        \li Displays the time when a binding is evaluated and the length of time
            the evaluation takes.
    \row
        \li \uicontrol {Signal handling}
        \li Displays the time when a signal is handled and the length of time the
            handling takes.
    \endtable

    \section1 Open as JSONC

    Select \uicontrol {{}} (\uicontrol {Open flame graph as a JSONC document})
    to open profiling data in JSONC format.

    \section1 Open in text editor

    Select \inlineimage {text-editor.png} {Open in text editor}
    (\uicontrol {Open in text editor}) to open \c {.qtd} files
    in XML format.

    \section1 Set project source and build folders

    To open QML source files when selecting events:

    \list 1
        \li Select \inlineimage {settings.png} {Settings}
            (\uicontrol {Open configuration settings}) to open the
            \uicontrol {Configuration} view.
            \image {vscodeext-qml-profiler-settings.webp} {QML Profiler settings}
        \li Select \inlineimage {folder.png} {Browse for a directory}
            (\uicontrol {Browse for a directory}), and then select source
            and build folders.
        \li Select \inlineimage {package.png} {Add workspace folders}
            (\uicontrol {Add workspace folders}) to add the currently open
            workspace folder.
        \li Select \uicontrol {Save}.
    \endlist

    To remove the folders from \uicontrol {Configuration}, select
    \inlineimage {clear.png} {Clear all entries} (\uicontrol {Clear all entries}).

    \sa {\QC: Profiling QML applications}
*/
